WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.480
A rule earns trust when it predicts experiments.

2
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.060
How would you test the two-signal gate?

3
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.570
Work in a slice of rat hippocampus.

4
00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.930
Stimulate the Schaffer collaterals, and record from CA1.

5
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:19.613
Give the input its usual fast train, 100 pulses per second.

6
00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:24.613
But hold one CA1 cell very negative, near minus −90 mV.

7
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:27.023
Will that cell's input potentiate?

8
00:00:32.617 --> 00:00:35.537
No, if the current keeps the active spines negative.

9
00:00:36.457 --> 00:00:40.877
Then the magnesium block stays strong, and glutamate arrives without the second

10
00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:41.850
signal.

11
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:47.270
In 1986, Malinow and Miller found that hyperpolarizing the cell during the train

12
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.910
prevented LTP, under their conditions.

13
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.230
The reverse also works.

14
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.470
Slow input, too weak to potentiate by itself, was paired with current that

15
00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:58.570
depolarized the cell.

16
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.850
That pairing produced LTP.

17
00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.770
Two groups reported it in 1986.

18
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.760
So which matters: how fast the input fires, or whether the cell is depolarized when

19
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:10.760
it does?

20
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.220
Coincidence. High frequency is not required.

21
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.900
A fast train is one way to make the cell supply its own depolarization.

22
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.900
Three experiments tested the gate directly.

23
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:28.800
You have just seen one.

24
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:30.783
First, block the receptor.

25
00:01:31.823 --> 00:01:37.363
In 1983, Collingridge, Kehl and McLennan applied the NMDA antagonist APV.

26
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:42.543
They used doses that abolished responses to an NMDA agonist.

27
00:01:43.243 --> 00:01:45.323
The baseline EPSP was unaffected.

28
00:01:46.017 --> 00:01:48.777
But a fast train no longer produced LTP.

29
00:01:49.837 --> 00:01:54.917
NMDA receptors were needed to start the change, not to carry the everyday response.

30
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.170
Second, block the calcium.

31
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:03.810
The same year, Lynch and colleagues injected the calcium buffer EGTA into CA1 cells.

32
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.420
LTP failed at those cells.

33
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.040
So induction needs a calcium rise inside the postsynaptic cell.

34
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.280
With the APV result, that points to calcium entering through NMDA receptors in

35
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.280
active spines.

36
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:20.173
One caution about scope.

37
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:23.053
Not every LTP uses this gate.

38
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:29.093
Mossy-fibre LTP, in guinea-pig CA3, needed no NMDA receptors.

39
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:35.767
The takeaway: block the NMDA receptor, the calcium, or the depolarization, and LTP

40
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:40.167
fails. Pair glutamate with enough depolarization, and it appears.
